1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,286
Five pinpricks of light
appeared on the dark Moon.

2
00:00:03,669 --> 00:00:06,327
Three astronauts
aboard NASA's Artemis

3
00:00:07,628 --> 00:00:09,718
Two mission marked
where they saw them.

4
00:00:10,043 --> 00:00:13,282
Orion was about forty-six
thousand miles away.

5
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:15,685
And at that point in the flyby,

6
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:18,622
the Moon was between
the spacecraft and Earth.

7
00:00:19,005 --> 00:00:20,213
Here's what the crew could see

8
00:00:20,259 --> 00:00:22,314
while Mission Control
was out of reach.

9
00:00:22,697 --> 00:00:24,659
When Orion moved
behind the Moon,

10
00:00:24,984 --> 00:00:28,061
the crew knew radio contact
with Earth would drop.

11
00:00:28,444 --> 00:00:30,998
The Moon itself blocked
the direct path.

12
00:00:31,439 --> 00:00:33,471
For a while,
the people aboard Orion

13
00:00:33,552 --> 00:00:35,375
could keep watching
and recording,

14
00:00:35,584 --> 00:00:39,264
while people on the ground
had to wait for contact to return.

15
00:00:39,868 --> 00:00:43,455
This was a rare chance to watch
the dark lunar surface.

16
00:00:44,268 --> 00:00:47,310
Without the bright face
of the Moon dominating the view,

17
00:00:47,589 --> 00:00:49,690
a short flash could stand out.

18
00:00:50,201 --> 00:00:52,175
The science team
had asked the astronauts

19
00:00:52,221 --> 00:00:53,742
to look for exactly that.

20
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:57,179
Picture a small piece
of rock moving through space.

21
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:01,196
At the Moon, there's no thick
atmosphere for it to cross.

22
00:01:01,637 --> 00:01:03,773
It can hit the surface
at high speed.

23
00:01:04,156 --> 00:01:07,651
A little of the impact energy
can appear as a brief flash.

24
00:01:07,976 --> 00:01:10,890
From Orion, that flash
was a pinprick.

25
00:01:11,331 --> 00:01:13,328
Now look at the annotations.

26
00:01:13,653 --> 00:01:16,521
Reid Wiseman marked
his observations in blue,

27
00:01:16,637 --> 00:01:20,294
Victor Glover in green,
and Jeremy Hansen in red.

28
00:01:20,805 --> 00:01:22,976
General annotations
are in purple.

29
00:01:23,359 --> 00:01:26,192
The drawing is their record
of where they saw light.

30
00:01:26,575 --> 00:01:30,407
It gives scientists something they
can compare across observers.

31
00:01:30,732 --> 00:01:32,403
An astronaut seeing a flash

32
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:35,457
and a camera capturing it
are two different things.

33
00:01:35,898 --> 00:01:38,766
The images give us
the crew’s view of the Moon.

34
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:41,924
Their notes tell us
where they saw the pinpricks.

35
00:01:42,365 --> 00:01:45,894
Put those together and you
can reconstruct what they reported,

36
00:01:46,103 --> 00:01:48,692
including cases where crew
members thought they

37
00:01:48,739 --> 00:01:50,294
had seen the same event.

38
00:01:50,898 --> 00:01:52,698
Once communications came back,

39
00:01:52,848 --> 00:01:55,089
the observations
could reach the ground.

40
00:01:55,472 --> 00:01:58,224
That's when the view
from Orion became evidence

41
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:01,939
the lunar science team
could examine alongside the rest

42
00:02:01,985 --> 00:02:03,344
of the flyby record.

43
00:02:03,948 --> 00:02:07,454
This is why scientists asked
a crew flying around the Moon

44
00:02:07,535 --> 00:02:10,310
to spend part of the trip
staring into darkness.

45
00:02:10,821 --> 00:02:13,897
A flash disappears almost
as soon as it arrives.

46
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,323
An observer who records where it
happened can leave a trace

47
00:02:17,357 --> 00:02:20,260
of the event long
after the light is gone.

48
00:02:20,643 --> 00:02:23,081
That makes these little
marks worth a second look.

49
00:02:23,464 --> 00:02:26,286
They show what three people
thought they saw in the dark,

50
00:02:26,611 --> 00:02:29,443
while the rest of us
were on the other side of the Moon.
